<h1 style=3D"margin-top:0;font-size:26px;">Paper Journal</h1>
<p>Hello Anna,</p>
<p>In this month=E2=80=99s edition, we compare feathering and drying across t=
hree paper stocks to help you choose a surface for your fountain pen.</p>
<p><strong>Fernleaf cotton</strong> showed the least feathering in our compar=
ison, making it worth a look if keeping ink edges tidy is your priority.</p>
<p><strong>Brook vellum</strong> dried most slowly. If you tend to turn pages=
 quickly or rest your hand near fresh writing, allow a little extra drying sp=
ace in your routine.</p>
<p>The takeaway: feathering and drying deserve separate attention. A paper th=
at suits deliberate journaling may feel different when you=E2=80=99re taking =
quick notes.</p>
<p>Try your usual pen and ink on a sample before settling on a stock, and con=
sider how you actually write=E2=80=94not just how the page looks.</p>
<p>Happy writing,<br>The Reed Paper team</p>
